research/frontier-2-judge.jsonl-183-{"id":"ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":false,"reason":"Fact [L6] claims that the canonical natural iota is injective, so that {iota(n) : n in N} is a countably infinite subset of R, and cites def-canonical-natural and def-natural-numbers. But def-canonical-natural explicitly states that injectivity is NOT part of the definition and is established by lem-of-naturals-positive instead: 'the order is what makes iota injective (lem-of-naturals-positive)' and 'injectivity is a lemma rather than part of the definition.' The cited items do not establish injectivity, so [L6] is stronger than its sources. The proof of claim 5 in step 2.2 depends on this unsupported claim: it needs {iota(n)} to be countably infinite (not merely finite) so that the sets A_n are at most countable and the cover {U_n} has no finite subcover. lem-of-naturals-positive is not in the item's dependency list and is not cited by [L6].","at":"2026-07-28T19:18:21.569Z"}
