research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-133-- **Wasserstein / transport metric** (statement level, if the course goes there).
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-134-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-135-### A.1.13 Function and sequence spaces
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-136-- **`B(X,Y)`** — bounded functions with the sup metric `d_∞(f,g) = sup_x d(f(x),g(x))`.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-137-- **`C(X)` / `C(X,Y)` / `C_b(X,Y)`** — continuous (bounded) functions with the sup metric.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-138-- **`C₀(X)`** — continuous functions vanishing at infinity.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-139-- **`ℓ^p`, `1 ≤ p ≤ ∞`** — `p`-summable sequences with `‖x‖_p`; `c`, `c₀`, `c₀₀`.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-140-- **`L^p(μ)`, `1 ≤ p ≤ ∞`** — as a quotient by a.e. equality.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md:141:- **`L^p` for `0 < p < 1`** — `d(f,g) = ∫|f−g|^p` is a metric but not a norm (unit ball non-convex).
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-142-- **Compact-open topology** and the topology of compact convergence on `C(X,Y)`.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-143-- **Sub-algebra, separating points, vanishing nowhere, self-adjoint sub-algebra, lattice** — the Stone–Weierstrass vocabulary.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-144-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-145----
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-146-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-147-## A.2 THEOREMS
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-148-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-149-### A.2.1 Basic metric geometry
--
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-307-- **Riesz–Fischer theorem** — `L^p(μ)` and `ℓ^p` are complete for `1 ≤ p ≤ ∞`.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-308-- **`ℓ^p` and `L^p[0,1]` are separable for `1 ≤ p < ∞`; `ℓ^∞`, `L^∞` are not.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-309-- **`ℓ²` is a Hilbert space; `L²` is a Hilbert space; every separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert space is isometrically isomorphic to `ℓ²`.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-310-- **`c₀` is a closed subspace of `ℓ^∞`; `c₀₀` is dense in `c₀` and in `ℓ^p` for `p < ∞`, not in `ℓ^∞`.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-311-- **For `0 < p < 1`, `d(f,g) = ∫|f−g|^p` is a translation-invariant metric that is not induced by a norm.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-312-- **`C[0,1]` with the sup metric is complete and separable; with the `L¹` metric it is neither complete nor closed.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-313-- **A linear map between normed spaces is continuous ⟺ bounded ⟺ Lipschitz.**
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-314-- **Every metric space isometrically embeds in `C_b(X)` (Kuratowski); every separable metric space isometrically embeds in `ℓ^∞`** (Fréchet).
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md:315:- **Kuratowski–Wojdysławski** — every bounded metric space is isometric to a closed subset of a convex subset of a Banach space.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-316-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-317----
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-318-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-319-## A.3 EXAMPLES (metric-specific, good library items)
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-320-
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-321-- **`ℝ^n` with `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_∞`** — Lipschitz equivalent, same topology, different balls (diamond / disc / square).
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-322-- **Discrete metric on any set** — every subset clopen, complete, bounded, totally bounded ⟺ finite, compact ⟺ finite.
research/sweep-metric-spaces-and-set-theory.md-323-- **`p`-adic metric on `ℚ`** — ultrametric; completion is `ℚ_p`; `ℤ_p` is compact, `ℚ_p` is locally compact and totally disconnected.
--
research/sweep-topology-core.md-153-- **Hewitt–Marczewski–Pondiczery**: a product of at most 2^κ spaces of density ≤ κ has density ≤ κ; in particular a product of at most 𝔠 separable spaces is separable — separability is far more productive than intuition suggests.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-154-- **Disjoint union**: each summand is clopen in ⨆Xᵢ; characteristic property (maps out of a sum = families of maps); ⨆ preserves local properties (local compactness, local connectedness) and destroys global ones (connectedness; compactness only for finite sums).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-155-- **Quotient theorems**: quotients preserve compactness, connectedness, path-connectedness, separability, Lindelöf; they do **not** preserve Hausdorffness, local compactness, first/second countability, or metrizability.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-156-- **Open equivalence relations**: if the quotient map q is open, then X/∼ is Hausdorff ⟺ the relation R ⊆ X×X is closed; X/∼ is T1 ⟺ every equivalence class is closed.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-157-- **Compact-Hausdorff quotient theorem**: if X is compact Hausdorff and R is a closed equivalence relation, X/R is compact Hausdorff; equivalently, a continuous surjection from a compact space onto a Hausdorff space is a quotient map — the standard tool for building the torus, Möbius band, Klein bottle and ℝPⁿ from squares/disks.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-158-- **Whitehead's theorem**: if q: X → Y is a quotient map and Z is locally compact Hausdorff, then q × id_Z : X×Z → Y×Z is a quotient map. **The product of two quotient maps need not be a quotient map** (Dowker's example).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-159-- **Adjunction space = pushout in Top**; if A is closed in Y, then X embeds as a closed subspace of X ∪_f Y and Y∖A embeds as an open subspace; adjunction of a normal space to a normal space along a closed set is normal.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-160-- **Cone/suspension**: CX is always contractible; ΣSⁿ ≅ Sⁿ⁺¹; X ↪ CX; the suspension is the double mapping cylinder of X → pt, X → pt.
research/sweep-topology-core.md:161:- **Order topology theorems**: any LOTS is Hausdorff and **hereditarily normal (completely normal)**, in fact monotonically normal; the order topology on a subset is generally **coarser** than the subspace topology, and they agree on convex subsets.
research/sweep-topology-core.md:162:- **Linear continuum ⟹ connected** (Munkres 24.1), and the connected subsets of a linear continuum are exactly the convex sets (intervals and rays).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-163-- **Ordinal spaces**: [0,λ] is compact for every ordinal λ; [0,ω₁) is countably compact, sequentially compact, first countable, but not compact and not Lindelöf; [0,ω₁] is compact but not first countable at ω₁ and not sequentially "reachable".
research/sweep-topology-core.md-164-- **Topological groups (elementary)**: translations and inversion are homeomorphisms ⟹ G is a **homogeneous space**; the topology is determined by any nbhd base at e; for each nbhd U of e there is a symmetric V with VV ⊆ U; **T0 ⟹ Hausdorff ⟹ completely regular (Tychonoff)**; the closure of a subgroup is a subgroup and of a normal subgroup is normal; an open subgroup is closed; G → G/H is always an **open** map; G/H is Hausdorff ⟺ H is closed; products of topological groups are topological groups; locally compact groups carry a Haar measure (statement level).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-165-- **Inverse limits**: an inverse limit of nonempty compact Hausdorff spaces over a directed index set is nonempty and compact Hausdorff (uses Tychonoff, hence AC); inverse limits of finite discrete spaces = profinite/Stone spaces.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-166-
research/sweep-topology-core.md-167-### SOURCE NOTES
research/sweep-topology-core.md-168-- **Munkres** splits the product topology in two: §15 (finite, X×Y) and §19 (arbitrary, product vs box) — pedagogically deliberate, and §19 is where the box-topology counterexamples live. Quotients are §22 and marked optional.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-169-- **Willard Ch. 3 "New Spaces from Old"** (§3.1 Subspaces, §3.2 Continuous functions, §3.3 Product spaces & weak topologies, §3.4 Quotient spaces) is the tightest four-section packaging of this whole topic; **Lee Ch. 3** has the identical title and covers the same ground with cell complexes to follow.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-170-- **Engelking Ch. 2 "Operations on topological spaces"** is the most complete: §2.1 Subspaces, §2.2 **Sums**, §2.3 Cartesian products, §2.4 Quotient spaces and quotient mappings, §2.5 **Limits of inverse systems**, §2.6 Function spaces I. He also supplies literal **invariance tables** in the appendix ("Invariants of operations", "Invariants and inverse invariants of mappings") — the single most useful curricular artifact for this section.
--
research/sweep-topology-core.md-205-- **Products**: an arbitrary product of connected spaces is connected (finite case by the "cross" argument; infinite case by density of finite-support sub-products — **requires AC to choose a base point in each factor**).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-206-- **Quotients and sums**: quotients of connected spaces are connected; a disjoint sum of ≥2 nonempty spaces is disconnected.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-207-- **Components**: partition X; are connected, maximal, and **always closed**; are *not* open in general; components are open ⟺ (locally, for each point) the space is locally connected.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-208-- **Quasicomponents**: each component ⊆ its quasicomponent; **components = quasicomponents in compact Hausdorff spaces (Šura-Bura)** and in locally connected spaces; the inclusion is strict in general (standard example: {0,1}×{0} ∪ ⋃ₙ({1/n}×[0,1]) style constructions).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-209-- **Path components ⊆ components**; path-connected ⟹ connected; **converse false** (topologist's sine curve).
research/sweep-topology-core.md-210-- **Locally connected ⟺ components of every open set are open** ⟺ the space is connected im kleinen at every point. (Pointwise, "connected im kleinen at x" is strictly weaker than "locally connected at x".)
research/sweep-topology-core.md-211-- **Locally path-connected ⟺ path components of every open set are open**; locally path-connected ⟹ components = path components.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-212-- **Connected + locally path-connected ⟹ path-connected.** Corollary: connected open subsets of ℝⁿ (and of manifolds) are path-connected, indeed polygonally connected.
research/sweep-topology-core.md:213:- **Connected subsets of ℝ are exactly the intervals** (including rays, ℝ, singletons, ∅); more generally the connected subsets of a linear continuum are exactly the convex sets.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-214-- **A linear continuum is connected**; ℝ is connected; [a,b] is connected.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-215-- **Cut point invariants**: homeomorphisms preserve cut points and the number of components of complements — used to prove (0,1) ≇ [0,1) ≇ [0,1], S¹ ≇ [0,1], ℝ ≇ ℝⁿ (n>1), and to distinguish letters/graphs.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-216-- **Sierpiński's theorem**: a continuum cannot be partitioned into countably many (≥2) pairwise disjoint nonempty closed sets.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-217-- **Boundary bumping lemma**: if C is a component of a closed proper subset K of a continuum X, then cl(C) ∩ ∂K ≠ ∅.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-218-- **Totally disconnected vs totally separated vs zero-dimensional**: totally separated ⟹ totally disconnected; zero-dimensional + T0 ⟹ totally separated; **for locally compact Hausdorff spaces all three coincide**; the counterexamples in general are the Knaster–Kuratowski fan and Erdős space.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-219-- **Stone spaces**: compact Hausdorff + totally disconnected ⟺ zero-dimensional compact Hausdorff ⟺ profinite (inverse limit of finite discrete spaces) ⟺ Stone dual of a Boolean algebra.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-220-- **Chain-connectedness**: connected ⟹ well-chained; the converse holds for **compact** metric spaces (ℚ is well-chained but not connected) — the standard bridge result usually left as an exercise.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-221-- **Local connectedness is preserved by**: open continuous surjections, quotient maps, disjoint sums, and finite products; **not** by arbitrary continuous maps or by arbitrary products (products of locally connected are locally connected iff all but finitely many factors are connected).
--
research/sweep-topology-core.md-363-12. **Whitehead's theorem**: (quotient map) × id_{locally compact Hausdorff} is a quotient map — **and the product of two quotient maps need not be a quotient map** (Dowker). This is the reason CGHaus exists.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-364-13. **Compact-to-Hausdorff trick**: any continuous surjection from compact onto Hausdorff is a closed map, hence a quotient map — the single most-used tool for identifying quotient constructions (torus, Klein bottle, ℝPⁿ) with familiar spaces.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-365-14. **Initial/final topology language and their transitivity**, with subspace/product as initial and quotient/sum as final — Bourbaki's organizing idea, almost always skipped in Anglophone first courses.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-366-15. **The embedding lemma / evaluation map** (separating points + separating points from closed sets ⟹ embedding into a product) — the shared engine of Tychonoff-cube embedding and Urysohn metrization.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-367-16. **Top's adjoint triple** discrete ⊣ forgetful ⊣ indiscrete, which explains why underlying sets of limits and colimits are the set-theoretic ones.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-368-
research/sweep-topology-core.md-369-**Products, orders, groups**
research/sweep-topology-core.md-370-17. **Box vs product**: f(t)=(t,t,t,…): ℝ → ℝ^ω continuous into the product, discontinuous into the box — and **cl(∏Aᵢ)=∏cl Aᵢ in *both*** topologies (the closure formula is not what distinguishes them).
research/sweep-topology-core.md:371:18. **The order topology on a subset ≠ the subspace topology in general**, with agreement on convex subsets — Munkres warns explicitly; almost everyone else buries it.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-372-19. **LOTS are hereditarily normal (completely normal), indeed monotonically normal** — a strong free theorem about ordered spaces.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-373-20. **Hewitt–Marczewski–Pondiczery**: products of ≤ 𝔠 separable spaces are separable — separability is vastly more productive than first/second countability.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-374-21. **Topological groups: T0 ⟹ Tychonoff, plus homogeneity and "topology determined by nbhds of e"** — three cheap, high-yield theorems most first courses never state.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-375-22. **Inverse limits of nonempty compact Hausdorff spaces are nonempty** — the fact that makes profinite/Stone theory work.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-376-
research/sweep-topology-core.md-377-**Connectedness**
research/sweep-topology-core.md-378-23. **Quasicomponents**, their containment of components, and the theorems that they coincide in compact Hausdorff (Šura-Bura) and in locally connected spaces — with an explicit strict example.
research/sweep-topology-core.md-379-24. **Locally connected ⟺ components of open sets are open**, and the pointwise gap between "connected im kleinen at x" and "locally connected at x".
--
